[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is arguably one of the coolest accessories you can buy for your DJI Pocket camera. It simply connects from the bottom and transmits a wireless video signal directly to the monitor. So now I can take my Pocket Camera with its built-in'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stabilizer, smart tracking, and very compact body and use it as a wireless camera in a multi- camera setup. So in this video, I\'ll show you some creative ways to use this'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transmitter and talk about a few limitations you should be aware of. Welcome back to Think Media, with you Nate. This is a wireless transmitter designed specifically for DJI Pocket cameras, made by a company called'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Vevor, Vevors, Virus? I don\'t know. I don\'t know how to pronounce it. Whatever it\'s called, I\'ll leave a link in the description below. This video is not sponsored. We bought this accessory ourselves, mainly because I really like it and I want'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to share it because I think it can really open up a lot of possibilities if you already have a Pocket Camera and want to use it for streaming or even live streaming. So, it works quite simply. It uses the'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'UVC webcam streaming mode on your DJI Pocket , and because this device is specifically designed for these cameras, it simply locks into place. It then takes the'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'webcam signal and transmits it to a separate receiver that I just connected to this monitor. And now I have a completely wireless system that I can move around with and shoot anything'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the go, with an incredibly small form factor. This is literally everything you need to get started. If you look closer at this transmitter, there are a few unique things that make it a great fit for'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'DJI Pocket cameras. You have a USB-C input if you want to supply power. There is also a slot for an NPF on the back panel, so you can power the transmitter that way. And they come in different sizes. I'

[01:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use the smaller NPF 550 battery. I managed to get about 3 hours of use out of one battery, which is fantastic. Moreover , it also powers the DJI camera itself.'

[02:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'As for the other ports on the device, you have HDMI input and output , a power button, and a few buttons to control various channels and settings. But in terms of work, everything is extremely simple.'

[02:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All I had to do was connect the DJI camera, make sure there was a battery, turn on the power, and everything worked flawlessly. I didn\'t have to worry about finding the right channel, the system did everything itself. Now let\'s take a look at the'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'receiver that I hid behind my little monitor - it\'s also pretty simple. You have an HDMI output that you can connect to a monitor or switcher. There is also the option of powering it with an'

[02:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'NP-F battery if you want to work remotely. But I prefer USB-C power since the device will likely be sitting next to a switch, so it can be kept plugged in for constant power.'

[02:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There\'s a power button on the side and a few buttons for channel control, but like I said, it\'s all very simple. There\'s also a USB-C UVC output, so you can connect it to a computer if the need arises. And'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'again, it is extremely simple to operate. I was very surprised by how simple it actually was. And that\'s probably what I like most about this whole system. Typically , if you want to'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'build a wireless camera for live streaming, you\'ll need a camera, a lens, a wireless transmitter, batteries, and maybe a stabilizer, and all of this quickly becomes bulky, inconvenient, and expensive.'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Compare this to the DJI Pocket , which has a stabilizer, lens, and a very compact body, and all you need is one accessory to transmit a wireless signal to a switch or'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'monitor. In addition, you have features such as tracking. You only need to double- tap and the camera will automatically follow the subject. So if someone is performing on stage, you just double-tap and the camera follows them'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', instead of having to do it manually. Since the camera is very easy to use, it can be given to someone without much photography experience and they will quickly figure it out. I think this kind of'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system is great for events or conferences. You can hand it to someone so that person can move around the room, broadcasting the image to the switch. For a church, this can be an additional angle for filming in the hall or'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even for actively following the pastor or speaker on stage . I\'m sure there are many more uses that I haven\'t even thought of, but once you understand the capabilities of the device, you can get pretty'

[04:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'creative. And of course, if you plan on using this for live broadcasts, you want to know how reliable it is. So, let\'s run some tests. Let\'s look at the delay. If I click my fingers'

[04:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right here, as you can see, there\'s little delay. With a wireless connection, there is always some delay, but the main thing is that it is not too long. I usually noticed a'

[04:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'delay of about four to five frames. So it\'s actually not that bad here. For the streaming work we\'re talking about, this is perfectly acceptable, but it\'s worth considering if you\'re trying to perfectly'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sync the device with other cameras. Here you can get a good idea of ​​the delay that occurs between the camera and the wireless signal . Next, we test the range. The manufacturer claims a'

[05:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fairly long distance, about 300 meters of line of sight, which is impressive, but I\'m more interested in how it works indoors, where there is not always perfect line of sight between the transmitter and receiver. So I did a quick test: I'

[05:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'left the receiver in the room and walked around the house, even going outside to check the stability of the connection. At this point, there is definitely no direct visibility. The signal passes through'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thick walls. So you may notice that the signal is interrupted from time to time. But even when I left the front of the house and walked further down the street, I was pleasantly surprised by how well the image held up . Of course, you\'re unlikely to'

[05:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take the transmitter outside while the receiver is inside. But if it\'s needed, I think the system will handle it, as long as you don\'t stray too far. You can transmit not only video, but also'

[06:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'audio. Another user tested this system and mentioned a slight clicking or popping sound in the receiver. So this is worth paying attention to. Honestly, most people will'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'probably only stream video using a separate audio system. Now let\'s talk about power. Both the transmitter and receiver are powered by Sony NP-F batteries, so you have plenty of options'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'depending on how long you need the device to run. I tested it with a Sony NP-F 550 battery with a capacity of about 25 Wh , and got about 3 hours and 6 minutes of operation, which is quite impressive. And'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of course, it will all depend on what size battery you are using. So, I think it\'s great that you can use such compact form factors to keep everything small and still last a long time. Another cool feature of the transmitter is that'

[06:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it also powers the pocket camera while in use. He actually implements it quite cleverly. After a certain amount of time, it stops charging the camera instead of keeping it charged all the time , which saves the'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transmitter battery, so you get a little more overall runtime since the pocket camera has its own internal battery. This is great because you don\'t have to worry about keeping your camera charged. The receiver can also be powered'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by an NP-F battery, but if it\'s next to a computer or video mixer, I \'d probably opt for USB-C power and leave it running. I should also note: if you want the transmitter to work all the time, you can connect'

[07:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'power via USB-C to hot- swap NP-F batteries. This helps ensure continuous operation, which is a very useful feature for long shoots. Another point worth paying attention to is heating.'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The transmitter becomes quite warm, especially if used for a long time . It gets really hot to the touch. I\'ve had this transmitter on for about an hour now, and I can clearly feel that it\'s become warm and a little uncomfortable'

[07:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to hold. So I would probably use some kind of handle on the bottom rather than holding the transmitter directly, because after a while it gets very uncomfortable. But other than that, I didn\'t notice any overheating issues when working'

[08:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'indoors. I guess if you\'re outside, it might be a little more disturbing. So be sure to get a handle or even mount it on a tripod if you plan on using it for hours. So far in this video I\'ve'

[08:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only used the Pocket 3 to demonstrate the connection, but let\'s move on to the Pocket 4 and 4P to see how they work. So, let\'s put away Pocket 3 and turn on Pocket 4'

[08:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right here. It automatically switched to webcam mode and we\'re ready to go. As you can see, the connection is great and you have'

[08:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'access to all the features of the Pocket 4 in webcam mode. A cool feature of using the Pocket 4 or 4P is that you can use the Frame Tap Remote to control the camera remotely.'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So maybe it\'s mounted on a tripod somewhere. You can just hold the frame, make sure everything is set up correctly, and even enable active tracking from the remote, which is pretty cool. So this is Pocket 4. Let\'s switch'

[09:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to 4P, and that\'s it. I have a 4P connected with the transmitter and we are still getting a signal, which is really cool. Of course, with the Pocket 4P you can'

[09:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use a camera with zoom. Here I am at 3x zoom. This is great if you are far away and want to zoom in on the image . You can even zoom up to 12x and still have active tracking.'

[09:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let\'s go to 3x zoom, turn on tracking, and see how it works. Isn\'t that incredible? Now, there are a few important points to understand about this setup.'

[09:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'First, it uses the Pocket webcam mode. So you won\'t necessarily get all the settings you would normally expect when recording on this camera. Another limitation is that the transmitter only supports 1080p resolution. So if you'

[10:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '\'re using a Pocket Camera and switch it to webcam mode, the maximum resolution will be 1080p. This isn\'t necessarily critical, as most streaming systems run at 1080p anyway, but'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you want to record on your camera while streaming, you\'re limited to 1080p resolution due to webcam mode. Another limitation specific to the Pocket 4 and 4P is'

[10:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that after a few minutes the screen goes black, you can\'t see the image and have to turn it back on, which is a bit annoying and I could n\'t find a setting to turn this off. If you'

[10:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go into the settings, this feature is blocked, which is quite annoying. This seems to have been changed in the 4 and 4P models, while the Pocket 3 does not have this problem. It just stays on'

[10:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all the time. And here\'s something else interesting: you\'re not technically limited to just these Pocket cameras. Essentially, it\'s just using the webcam\'s UVC signal. So with a short USB-C extension cable, you'

[11:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can connect other cameras, such as DJI\'s Action series. You\'ll have to figure out a way to secure the camera and this transmitter together, but the cool thing is that this can be used with other cameras. And I know someone will ask, "'

[11:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Can I use the Insta360 Luna Pro or Ultra with this transmitter?" The answer is no. At this time, these cameras do not support such a UVC connection. And there\'s a different mount'

[11:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on top, so it won\'t work as easily as with pocket cameras. I would be happy if Insta360 would enable UVC support in the future, or for another company to release an adapter specifically for Luna. But for now, you'

[11:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'should definitely use a pocket camera with this transmitter. So , if you were to ask me, "Who is this wireless transmitter for?" I definitely don\'t think every owner of such a camera needs it. But many of us already have these'

[11:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cameras and are involved in streams, events, conferences, or church broadcasts. I find this a really interesting accessory. You can buy it for less than $300 plus the cost of a pocket'

[12:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera. This is much cheaper and, perhaps, more functional than expensive traditional cameras, which require a separate transmitter setup. It is much more expensive and significantly more difficult to set up.'

[12:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Overall, I think this is a really cool accessory that opens up new possibilities for pocket cameras. But I would be interested to know how you plan to use something like this. Let us know in the comments below. If you\'re still hesitating between the DJI Pocket 4'

[12:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and 4P, watch this screencast video where I compare them in detail.'
